# AI Energy Calculator

**What did your AI session cost the planet?**

A 30-second interactive tool that estimates the energy footprint of generative AI sessions: images, video, audio, text, vibe-coded sessions, and 3D models. Built for storytellers who want to create consciously.

🌐 **Live app:** https://aienergycalculator.lovable.app

## How it works

1. Tell it what you made and how much
2. Pick the platform you used
3. Get your estimated energy use, translated into things you can actually feel: smartphone charges, fridge days, steaks cooked

## Methodology

All figures are midpoint estimates drawn from published research. Nothing is invented. Where peer-reviewed data doesn't exist yet, that's flagged in the app.

| Content type | Energy per unit | Source basis |
|---|---|---|
| AI image (standard) | 1.7 Wh per image | WSJ / Hugging Face / Carnegie Mellon |
| AI video, SD (6-8s clip) | 65 Wh per clip | Hugging Face midpoint (20-110 Wh range) |
| AI video, 720p HD (8s) | 90 Wh per clip | CNET / Hugging Face (~90 Wh avg) |
| AI video, 1080p FHD (8s) | 110 Wh per clip | Upper range, Hugging Face |
| AI video, 4K | 150 Wh per clip | Extrapolated upper bound, flagged as estimate |
| AI-generated audio added to video | Clip total x 1.3 | ~30% overhead per source data |
| AI audio only | 20 Wh per minute | Midpoint of 10-30 Wh/min range |
| AI text, simple prompt | 0.17 Wh per prompt | WSJ (lightweight Llama, single GPU) |
| AI text, complex reasoning | 1.7 Wh per prompt | WSJ (large multi-GPU Llama) |
| Vibe coding session | 1,250 Wh per hour | Midpoint of 500-2,000 Wh/hr range |
| 3D model | 1.7 Wh per model | Estimated at image-generation equivalent, flagged |

Results are contextualized using these equivalencies:

| Equivalency | Conversion |
|---|---|
| Smartphone charge | 1 full charge = 10 Wh |
| Refrigerator day | 1 day = 40 Wh |
| LED bulb hour | 1 hour = 9 Wh |
| Mile driven (gas car) | 1 mile = 330 Wh |
| Minute of TV (65" OLED) | 1 min = 2.43 Wh |
| Steak cooked (electric grill) | 1 steak = 220 Wh |

## A note on the numbers

These are estimates, not measurements. Exact figures vary by model, hardware, and energy mix. Treat this as a conversation starter, not a scientific instrument.

